Key requirements from the test cases:

1. Numbers (including negative numbers) are pushed onto the stack.
2. `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` are binary operations, with stack underflow errors.
3. Division by zero is an error.
4. `DUP`, `DROP`, `SWAP`, `OVER` are case-insensitive.
5. Word definitions: `: name body ;` — definitions are case-insensitive, and user-defined words can override built-ins.
6. "Can use words with different names" — local scoping: when defining `: bar foo ;`, the `foo` inside the definition refers to the `foo` at the time of definition, not the later redefinition. This is the "local scope" test mentioned in the commit message.
7. "Can define a word that uses a word with the same name" — `: foo 10 ;`, `: foo foo 1 + ;` — the `foo` inside the second definition refers to the old definition of `foo` (the one that pushes 10). This is also local scope.
8. Cannot redefine a number (`: 1 2 ;` is an error).
9. Executing a non-existent word is an error.

The test only checks whether the error is nil or non-nil (the `explainText` field is not checked in forth_test.go — let me verify: `if err == nil { ... } else if tc.expected != nil { ... }`. The test does not check the error message. Good, so the exact error message doesn't matter.

