package forth

import (
	"errors"
	"strconv"
	"strings"
)

type forth struct {
	stack []int
	words map[string]func(*forth) error
}

func (f *forth) push(v int) error {
	f.stack = append(f.stack, v)
	return nil
}

func (f *forth) pop() (int, error) {
	if len(f.stack) == 0 {
		return 0, errors.New("empty stack")
	}
	v := f.stack[len(f.stack)-1]
	f.stack = f.stack[:len(f.stack)-1]
	return v, nil
}

func (f *forth) binary(op func(a, b int) (int, error)) func(*forth) error {
	return func(f *forth) error {
		b, err := f.pop()
		if err != nil {
			return err
		}
		a, err := f.pop()
		if err != nil {
			return err
		}
		v, err := op(a, b)
		if err != nil {
			return err
		}
		return f.push(v)
	}
}

// ...
```

The local scope issue: when defining `: bar foo ;`, the tokens of the definition body should be resolved against the word dictionary at the time of definition, so that later redefinitions of `foo` don't affect `bar`.

Approach: when parsing the definition, resolve each token of the body into a closure (or a list of operations) immediately, so that the body is a list of resolved operations. Then `bar` executes the resolved operations. This handles both "can use words with different names" and "can define a word that uses a word with the same name".

Wait, but there's a subtlety with "can define a word that uses a word with the same name": `: foo 10 ;` defines foo → push 10. Then `: foo foo 1 + ;` — the `foo` in the body refers to the *old* foo (push 10), then `1 +` → 11. With immediate resolution, when parsing the body of the second `: foo ... ;`, the dictionary still has the old foo, so the body resolves to [push 10, push 1, +] → 11. ✓

"Can use words with different names": `: foo 5 ;` → foo = [push 5]. `: bar foo ;` → bar = [old foo = push 5]. `: foo 6 ;` → foo = [push 6]. `bar foo` → bar pushes 5, foo pushes 6 → [5, 6]. ✓

"Can override built-in words": `: swap dup ;` → dictionary["swap"] = [dup]. `1 swap` → [1,1]. ✓

"Definition is case-insensitive": `: SWAP DUP Dup dup ;` — the name "SWAP" is lowercased to "swap" and overrides the built-in swap with the body [dup, dup, dup]. `1 swap` → [1,1,1,1]. ✓

